{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"d6e46f73064befcc","type":"text","text":"## Factors affecting investors needs","x":-140,"y":-40,"width":240,"height":100},
		{"id":"74757750b33dca6c","type":"text","text":"## Investment Objectives\n\n### Required Return\n\n* **Goal-Driven Calculation**: required rate of return **calculated** from **current assets**, **expected** **savings** and **target** future **wealth**.\n\n* **Real vs Nominal Returns**: required returns should be **adjusted** for **inflation** (real) to preserve purchasing power.\n\n* Total Return vs Income Focus: total return perspective makes no distinction between capital gains and income (dividends/interest). Others separate capital gains for long term growth and income for current spending.\n\n* Risk-return trade-off: Higher expected returns requires greater risk. If goal unachievable with risk tolerance, goals or savings must be adjusted\n\n### Risk Tolerance\n\nAn investors risk tolerance determined by two components\n\n1. **Ability** to Take Risk: financial **measurement** based clients **assets**, **liabilities**, **liquidity** **needs** and t**ime horizon**. Investors with more assets vs liabilities or **longer time horizons** have more ability to **absorb** temporary **market losses**.\n2. **Willingness** to Take Risk: **phycological** measurement based on clients **attitude** to risk, **investment experience**.\n\nIf investors willingness and ability are not the same **advisor** should recommend the **lesser of the two**.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","x":-1120,"y":-550,"width":880,"height":670},
		{"id":"2c1596e69986407c","type":"text","text":"## Investment Constraints\n\n### Time Horizon and Longevity\n\n* Time Horizon: the shorter the time horizon the less risk required and need highly liquid investments.\n* Longevity Risk: risk an individual outlives their assets due to increased life expectancy.\n\n### Liquidity Needs\n\n* **Individual**: Required for emergency **reserves** or **planned** future **expenses** (**accident**, **tuition** fees).\n* **Institutional**: driven by incoming vs outgoing liabilities (e.g. **pension** **fund** needs cash when **benefit** **payouts** exceed contributions)\n\n### Taxes\n\n* Tax treatment can vary by investor type and asset class.\n* Pension funds often tax exempt.\n* sometimes dividends taxed more than capital growth.\n\n#### Regulatory Constraints\n\n* Certain investors face regulatory constraints on their portfolios\n\t* Insurance companies face regulation to protect policy holders.\n\t* Some institutional funds restricted in their holdings of overseas assets.\n\n\n#### Unique Circumstances\n\n* ESG and Impact Investing: incorporating environmental, social and government risk into investment decisions, targeting positive societal impacts.\n* Religious and Ethical: for example Islamic law or a medical foundation not investing in tobacco.\n* Single Company Exposure: Limiting employers stick to prevent concentrated risk.\n","x":200,"y":-550,"width":840,"height":670},
		{"id":"817862529b9b3517","type":"text","text":"## Behavioural\n\nNote that behavioural finance studies how human psychology and emotions cause investors to deviate from logical decision making","x":-235,"y":-880,"width":430,"height":160},
		{"id":"4e1623950ad268ba","x":-320,"y":480,"width":600,"height":370,"type":"text","text":"## IPS\n\nInvestment Policy Statement is a written planning document specifying an investors objectives and constraints.\n\n### Institutional IPS and Governance\n\nAs well as the above objectives and constraints:\n\n* **Connects** investment **targets** to overall **mission**.\n* Explicitly lists **permitted** **asset** **classes** and **allocation percentages**.\n* **Restrictions** regarding **leverage** and **short selling**.\n* **Performance Benchmarks** - financial market **indices** total portfolio returns will be **evaluated** against.\n* Defines structures and **authority** of investment **committee**, **outsource** vs **insourced**.\n* **Annually** reviewed or immediately if **changed** **circumstances**.\n\n"}
	],
